1 OCCU Sølvsmed og urmaker som faren. Innførte poteten til Telemark.(Telemark Husflidslag 1924-1974 side 58), Gullsmed i Kragerø, Engelbret ( Ingebreth, Ingebræt osv.) Resen ( Reisner), Han er vel uppkalla etter farmori, Engel
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Engelbret Resen Mikkelsson Mandt was one of the most intriguing members of the
2 CONT
2 CONT family. Engelbret’s true story far surpasses the legends we always accepted as fact. Let
2 CONT
2 CONT me set the stage: he was born in 1722 and was baptized in Brevik. Engelbret was raised
2 CONT
2 CONT as a farmer but also became an accomplished silver and gold craftsman. He was a wellread
2 CONT
2 CONT and learned man, although without formal education. Engelbret traveled in
2 CONT
2 CONT Norway and Europe buying minerals and marketing his wares. He spoke German,
2 CONT
2 CONT Dutch, and English besides Norwegian, and could read English, German, and
2 CONT
2 CONT Norwegian. He had black hair, black eyes, and often wore a brown wig. (Mandt, O.L.)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an apprentice goldsmith in Kragerø to Ole Olsen, the most dominating jeweler
2 CONT
2 CONT in Kragerø at the time. After Ole Olsen died, Engelbret married Ole’s widow Anna
2 CONT
2 CONT Catharina, taking over the clients, the business, and the home of his former teacher and
2 CONT
2 CONT partner, a common practice of those days. Anna Catharina was nineteen years older
2 CONT
2 CONT than Engelbret’s twenty-two years, and this was her third marriage. She had a son,
2 CONT
2 CONT Bendix Jahn, from her first marriage, a daughter, Maren Olesdatter, from her second,
2 CONT
2 CONT but she and Engelbret were childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbret served his community of Kragerø during the 1740s and 1750s: he was a
2 CONT
2 CONT tax appraiser in 1748; and in 1749, he and his brother-in-law Frantz Cudrio were jury
2 CONT
2 CONT members. Engelbret was a frequent legal witness in church affairs and important civil
2 CONT
2 CONT events. Ambros Sollid wrote in Telemark Landsbruksselskap (Telemark Agriculture)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1777-1877, “The first time we hear of potato growing in Telemark is in 1758 by
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingebrigt Resen in Mo.” (Sollid, A.) This is confirmed by Professor S. Hasund in an
2 CONT
2 CONT article in “Norsk Hydro”, No. 5, 1946. It cannot be proved that he introduced potatoes
2 CONT
2 CONT to Norway, as it was written earlier, but it is evident that he was the one to first
2 CONT
2 CONT introduce them to Upper Telemark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our family has always accepted that while on a marketing trip, ca. 1764-68 to
2 CONT
2 CONT Copenhagen, and perhaps England and Germany, he was robbed and killed. Engelbret’s
2 CONT
2 CONT brothers, Olav and Rasmus, tried to discover what had become of him, placing ads in
2 CONT
2 CONT European newspapers with his description. Two of Olav’s sons, Mikkel and Tarjei,
2 CONT
2 CONT traveled to England to claim their uncle’s property from the authorities. They were
2 CONT
2 CONT shown a grave that could have been his but were not allowed to claim possessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olav Solberg proved in 1989 that Engelbret did not die after an attack somewhere in
2 CONT
2 CONT Europe, but lived for nearly twenty more years. He died in 1781 and is buried in the
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikolaj church cemetery, in Copenhagen. This church was destroyed by fire in 1795,
2 CONT
2 CONT but the records are intact. He was buried 11 June 1781; the 185th body buried that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Nikolaj parish)
2 CONT
2 CONT During the years of Engelbret’s disappearance, from 1764 and until his death, he
2 CONT
2 CONT used the name Engelbret Michaelsen and claimed to be almost six years younger. He
2 CONT
2 CONT lived most of these years in Copenhagen. He was a merchant and legal distiller of
2 CONT
2 CONT 8
2 CONT
2 CONT © 1997, 2005 All Rights Reserved by Lorna Mandt Robertson Email: ljmr27@comcast.net
2 CONT
2 CONT “Heglands on the Web” (http://heglands.tripod.com) has received permission and the exclusive right to
2 CONT
2 CONT post “The Mandts of Upper Telemark” on the Internet by its author and owner, Lorna Mandt Robertson.
2 CONT
2 CONT No part of “The Mandts of Upper Telemark” may be reproduced on the Internet or using any electronic
2 CONT
2 CONT media without the prior written consent of the author.
2 CONT
2 CONT spirits. He became a citizen of Copenhagen and a member of the distiller’s guild. He
2 CONT
2 CONT owned property, and although he became engaged not long before his death, he did not
2 CONT
2 CONT marry.
2 CONT
2 CONT He never contacted his family or attempted to claim money or property in Norway,
2 CONT
2 CONT even after the death of Anna Catharina. By 1777, he had written a manuscript titled
2 CONT
2 CONT Historisk Beskrivelse Over Øvre Tellemarken (Historic Description of Upper
2 CONT
2 CONT Telemark), and this work was given to Fredrik, the Danish crown prince, (1753-1805).
2 CONT
2 CONT The manuscript today is in the Royal Copenhagen Library in good condition after 200
2 CONT
2 CONT years, frequently read and referred to for historical, mythological, and topographical
2 CONT
2 CONT information on Upper Telemark.
2 CONT
2 CONT This family owes so much to Olav Solberg for his bringing to light the true story of
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbret. We not only have the truth, which is better than fiction, but Engelbret’s own
2 CONT
2 CONT words to read and enjoy. Engelbret wrote with great charm and detail about Upper
2 CONT
2 CONT Telemark. He must have loved its valleys, streams, and peaks. We are the heirs to this
2 CONT
2 CONT unique treasure!
2 CONT
2 CONT From Olav Solberg's 1989 book Engelbret Michaelsen Resen Mandt: Historisk
2 CONT
2 CONT Beskrivelse Over Øvre Tellemarken: "The name Resen (also written Reisen, Reysener,
2 CONT
2 CONT Resner, and Reisner) is Danish and comes from the name of a village by the same name
2 CONT
2 CONT (Resen) in Skodborg district, earlier called Ribe, today known as Viborg on Jylland
2 CONT
2 CONT (Jutland), the peninsula.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT - Book - Engelbret Michaelsen Resen Mandt: Historisk Beskrivelse Over Øvre Tellemarken - Olav Solberg, 1989 - https://www.nb.no/items/f531e48f50e7fed3aef14627624ea675?page=0
