1 OCCU Bodde pa Nedre Omli da faren døde hos ham i aug.1766. Drev med sølvsmedarbeid og sagbruk. Lensmann i Fyresdal1771-79, Lensmann i Fyresdal, døypt 1724 i Gjerpen, lensmann i Skafså
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bodde pa Nedre Omli i august 1766 - da hans far døde hos ham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Drev med sølvsmedarbeid og sagbruk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 15.12.1777 tilladt aarlig at ski‘re 1000de Bord dog kuns av tømmer av hans egne Skove" Peter var lensmann i Fyresdal 1771-79 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tilsammen hadde han 12 barn, men 4 døde tidlig - en av de het Else.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han kjøpte bla Eidsborg stavkyrke i dødsboet etter Christian von Ross, men solgte den seinere til menigheten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Petter Kristian Mikkelsson Mandt (Peter Christian) was born 1724, and was baptised July 9, 1724 in Gjerpen church near Skien. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Åse Tarjeisdatter Loftsgarden, Byrte, born 1731. The marriage date is unavailable, as there was no church register kept between 1748 and 1766, but they must have married before 1752. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a trained silversmith and a farmer. He bought one of the Håtveit farms in Mo about 1750 and lived there until about 1752 when he bought Floten in Mo. He traded Floten for one of the Dalen farms in Eidsborg. In 1761, Petter 
2 CONC traded the Dalen farm for Førstøyl in
2 CONT
2 CONT Høydalsmo. He lived there until 1762, when he returned to Romdal in Eidsborg. In only one year he traded for Nedre Åmli (Nigard) in Skafså, and that was where he lived the remainder of his life. (Mandt, K.O.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Petter Kristian and Åse had nine children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was lensmann of Fyresdal 1777-1779, and documented much of the history of that time. He owned the only sawmill in West Telemark by royal privilege, and owned extensive forest lands. He farmed and dealt in real estate, buying an
2 CONC d selling property. His penmanship was clear, and Petter Kristian
2 CONT
2 CONT prepared documents locally and in neighboring areas. (Mandt, O.L.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Petter Kristian died in 1782, when he was only 59, and was buried in Skafså churchyard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Petter Kristian, and his connection to Eidsborg stave kirke were an important part of our family history. This stave church was built about 1250. After the  reformation in 1546, the Crown confiscated everything belonging to the ol
2 CONC d Catholic church.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Royal decree in 1721, the churches in Norway were sold to private people.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eidsborg church was then sold to a high-ranking officer. When this officer died, Petter Kristian together with other members of the local congregation purchased the church at a public auction in 1759, and later turned it back to t
2 CONC he people. (Mandt, K.O.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT His great grandson, Mikkel Pettersson Mandt, painted the altar nearly 100 years later. The Eidsborg church contained a carved wooden image of St. Nicolaus of Bari, the patron saint of this church, dating from the 11th century. In 
2 CONC earlier times, on St. John's Day,
2 CONT
2 CONT parishioners carried St. Nicolaus down the hill to a little lake, where they bathed him to free them of their sins and to insure good crops. By the mid-1800s the custom was abandoned, and Petter Mikkelsson Mandt, lensmann and poet
2 CONC , grandson of Petter Kristian, carried the carved image to Oslo, presenting it to the Oslo University Collection of Antiquities in 1850, where it is today. Today a replica rests in a corner of
2 CONT
2 CONT the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
